Well, just for starters, one neverending nightmare
with MS-Windows is that it allows blanks in file
names, but also treats blanks as delimiters, so
almost every file pathname has to be in quotes,
including ones to libraries of whose location you
might well be ignorant. Then, Unix, Linux, and GNU
stuff expect two flavors of quotes to work, but
MS-Windows only recognizes double quotes. Then, the
MS-Windows file pathname separator backslashes are
escape characters to both *nix and to the C language,
so what you think is a pair of characters might end
up being one character, like \n, and that being a
whitespace character, and thus a delimiter. Unix and
MS-Windows use different wildcard characters; they
us different scripting languages, yadda, yadda, pain.

This is just the easy ugly sins Bill Gates committed
up front.

It got worse when he started adding build tools.

The online rants covering all the things wrong with
MS-Windows as a development environment fill disk
farms. You _really_ don't want to hear them all.

